  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1223 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nMonday looks to trend as the warmest day of the week with greater \nafternoon sunshine, even with the mid-level ridging sliding to the \neast with the influence from an approaching storm system over the \nIntermountain West. This storm system advances into the Central \nPlains into Tuesday and this sends a cold front into our area. The \nfront looks on target to arrive sometime from the overnight into \nTuesday morning before exiting the Coastal Plains around early \nafternoon. The front has a continued signal of bringing at least a \nbroken line of showers and storms immediately along it across the \nmajority of the region. There could be enough deep layer shear \ncombined with limited instability for some organized stronger \nconvection along the line. The rain and storm chances then quickly \nshutoff behind the front as moderate northwesterly winds help to \nusher drier air into the region. This drier air helps yield to \ncooler mornings for mid to late week and mild but refreshing \nafternoons with lower humidity. Changes may be then on the horizon \nentering the weekend pending the amount of moisture with onshore \nflow and the evolution of the upper level pattern.",
